course: dictionary
lesson: 3277
title: Error: a mistake
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An error is a mistake, something done wrongly. Errors happen to everyone, even careful writers and scientists. At school, you find and correct errors in your spelling, punctuation and sums.
explain: Errors can be small, like a missing full stop, or big, like a wrong answer that changes a whole project. Computers show an error message when something has gone wrong. «Rust's compiler is strict, but its error messages often explain the problem and suggest a fix.»
explain: Finding an error is a skill. Read your work again slowly, and ask: does this make sense? Then try the fix and check the result, because a fix can also have errors.
fact: An error is a mistake, something done wrongly.
fact: In maths and science, an error is the difference between a measured or estimated value and the true value.
fact: A computer error is a problem that stops a program working correctly.
fact: Useful phrases: make an error, by error (by mistake), trial and error, human error.
fact: «Searching for the exact error message often finds someone who solved the same problem, but not every answer online is right.»
exercise: Sam wrote 7 x 8 = 54 in his book. What is the error here? | The answer is right and very neat | The book is too old | Sam used the wrong colour of pen | *The answer is wrong, it should be 56
exercise: «Searching for the exact error message often finds someone who solved the same problem.» What is an error message? | *A message that tells you something went wrong | A tune the computer plays | A letter to say sorry | A message from a friend about your birthday
exercise: The parcel was sent to the wrong house by error. What does by error mean? | *By mistake | Inside a box | Very quickly | On purpose
test: Which sentence shows an error in science? | The laboratory was painted blue last year. | The scientist's measurement was 2 cm off the true length, so there was an error in her result. | The scientist went to the park on Sunday. | *The scientist was happy with her lunch today.
test: Ava says, «I made an error, so I need to check my answers again.» What does Ava mean? | She won a prize for her answers | She lost her pencil case | *She made a mistake and wants to fix it | She finished her homework very early
exam: «Code can run without errors and still be the wrong idea, like a door that needs a key the player can never get; playtesting finds such problems.» What does this tell you about errors? | Code with no error messages is always correct | Playtesting is never needed | *A program can run without error messages and still be wrong, so testing is needed | Errors only happen in games
